        <mxCell id="leg" parent="1" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#D6DAE3;fontColor=#1B2A4A;fontSize=14;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=8;spacingTop=4;arcSize=7;" value="&lt;b&gt;Reading the picture&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size:11px&quot; color=&quot;#3a4a66&quot;&gt;The spine is the golden thread: Outcome, Platform, Capability, Bounded context, ABB, SBB, Service, extended here with the landscape above it and the node below it.&lt;br&gt;Platform is the unit of ownership and consumption; domain is the problem space it serves. The two usually coincide and are never the same thing.&lt;br&gt;An ABB lives inside one bounded context: this bundle&#39;s rule, where TOGAF leaves a block enterprise-wide. Reuse is through a pattern that names it, or a cross-cutting platform.&lt;br&gt;Architecture pattern and reference architecture are two types of one construct (Kruchten, Bass). A pattern&#39;s abstraction is read from its boxes; the ladder reads it: R3 logical, R4 physical.&lt;br&gt;A service is the C4 container that executes code: separately built, versioned, released and deployed, inside one bounded context, run by a product, deployed to nodes.&lt;br&gt;Own conventions: the rung, the consideration, product runs service, store as a resource (as Backstage). The rest: TOGAF 10, O-AA, DDD, Team Topologies, CNCF, C4, Backstage, ArchiMate 3.2, the cloud Well-Architected frameworks.&lt;br&gt;Solid arrow: a relationship recorded in front matter. Dashed arrow: may point at any level.&lt;/font&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="150" width="1360" x="20" y="742" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
